---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import { SITE_TITLE } from "../consts";

const description =
    "About José María Sánchez Salas — Software Engineer and Architect focused on complex software systems.";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
    <head>
        <BaseHead title={`About — ${SITE_TITLE}`} description={description} />
    </head>

    <body>
        <Header />

        <main>
            <section class="page-intro">
                <h1>I LIKE UNDERSTANDING HOW THINGS WORK</h1>

                <p class="lead">
                    Not just the code, but the whole thing: what the system is
                    trying to achieve, what constraints it has, where the pieces
                    connect, and what happens when something inevitably goes
                    wrong.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I'm José María, and I've spent most of my career building
                    and evolving software systems in very different
                    environments.
                </p>
            </section>

            <section class="page-section">
                <h2>THE ENVIRONMENTS CHANGED. THE PROBLEM DIDN'T</h2>

                <p>
                    I've worked on backend platforms, APIs, distributed
                    processing systems and real-time data.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I've also worked much closer to the physical world:
                    industrial edge systems running on constrained hardware,
                    communicating through different industrial protocols and
                    deployed at customer sites.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Those environments have very different constraints. But they
                    share something important.
                </p>

                <p class="statement">
                    <strong>
                        There is usually more complexity than can be understood
                        by looking at one piece of the system at a time.
                    </strong>
                </p>
            </section>

            <section class="page-section">
                <h2>THAT'S WHERE I LIKE TO WORK</h2>

                <p>
                    I enjoy figuring out how the pieces actually fit together.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Where the boundaries should be. Which constraints are real.
                    What needs to happen in real time. What happens when
                    something fails. Which abstractions are useful and which
                    ones are just adding another layer of complexity.
                </p>

                <p>
                    That often means going deeper than the initial problem
                    suggests.
                </p>

                <p>
                    A performance problem might actually be a data-model
                    problem. A scaling problem might actually be a
                    workload-management problem. A messy codebase might actually
                    be suffering from unclear boundaries.
                </p>

                <p class="statement">
                    <strong
                        >I like finding the problem underneath the problem.</strong
                    >
                </p>
            </section>

            <section class="page-section">
                <h2>ARCHITECTURE WITHOUT LEAVING ENGINEERING BEHIND</h2>

                <p>
                    Over time, my work moved from implementing individual
                    services to taking more responsibility for how larger
                    systems fit together.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I've designed systems from scratch, evolved existing
                    architectures, investigated difficult performance problems,
                    changed database models, worked with constrained hardware
                    and integrated systems that don't behave like ordinary
                    software.
                </p>

                <p>
                    I've also led small engineering teams and worked closely
                    with other engineers to turn ambiguous problems into
                    something concrete enough to build.
                </p>

                <p>I still write code.</p>

                <p class="statement">
                    <strong>
                        For me, architecture is about making better engineering
                        decisions before — and while — the code is being
                        written.
                    </strong>
                </p>
            </section>

            <section class="page-section">
                <h2>WHAT KEEPS ME INTERESTED</h2>

                <p>I like learning how unfamiliar systems work.</p>

                <p>
                    Understanding something I didn't build. Finding the
                    assumption nobody questioned. Tracing a problem through
                    several layers until the real cause becomes visible.
                </p>

                <p>
                    And then making the whole thing a little simpler than it was
                    before.
                </p>
            </section>
        </main>

        <Footer />
    </body>
</html>
